text:
@jasonlk The 2020 spike distorted how everyone reads @Zoom.

Strip it out and you get the honest SaaS curve: 15 years of compounding with 2 loud ones.

The quiet years build the $27B.

@PrismML Local models change what you can promise a customer.

Offline, private, no usage anxiety.

Whole product categories just became viable on hardware people already own.

@ArtificialAnlys Watch the $0.34 per task, that's the story.

We run automation for 1M  sellers.

At that scale the model bill beats the model IQ every single month.

@AnatoliKopadze First 2 years of my startup, I set a daily 3 AM alarm to restart our servers by hand.

I was the self-improving loop.

Watching agents run for hours on their own feels personal.

@Rasmic Shipping this fast changes what validation means.

I used to charge real money for products that didn't exist yet, refund, and only build the winners.

Now the build costs less than the test.

@0xMoysei Scaling to 250 employees taught me the same thing.

When someone failed, I'd usually find a broken process behind it.

Agents just made that lesson impossible to ignore.

@jaredrhod Same pattern across 9 years of building.

100% of my side projects failed. 100% of the focused ones worked.

One agent you fully understand beats 40 you babysit.

@HowToPrompt__ The most underrated line here is 'runs on plain SQLite.'

My first SaaS had no website for 3 years. A logo and a login screen.

Boring wins more often than anyone admits.

@gregisenberg I built @AutoDS because I needed it myself, running 100 eBay stores.

The first big agent startups will get built the same way.

By people who run agents all day and hit the walls first.

@gregisenberg Owning your setup beats chasing the best model.

When my AI billing got split this year, I swapped my whole stack in 10 minutes because it was file-based.

Build so you can switch. Don't get locked in.

@Aidanb2b The founder as the face is underrated.

I once reached out to 30 influencers. 2 replied, 1 stood me up.

So I became the channel myself. Nobody sells your thing as you do.

@tbvxyz Per-seat dying is the real story here.

You used to charge for access.

Now you charge for outcomes. The teams that learn to price the result instead of the login win the next 5 years.

@aryanlabde Plenty are building it.

The problem is marketing isn't a tooling problem.

No tool fixes a product nobody wants. Get 10 people to actually pay first, then worry about the stack.

@tylerrwayne Replacing your stack was never the hard part.

Getting people to pay you is.

No marketing site for the first 2-3 years of @AutoDS. Just word of mouth. Tools change. That part never does.

@mikefutia Built my own tools for years.

Spent the first 2 years of @AutoDS restarting servers at 3 am because of it.

Building it yourself isn't free. You just pay with your time instead of a subscription.

@zach_yadegari We've acquired a few companies over the years.

The ones worth buying were never the ones with the prettiest product.

They already solved one painful problem for one specific group. Revenue follows that.

@AnatoliKopadze Prompting won't disappear in 6 months.

The skill just moves up a level.

Knowing what to build and why has always been the real bottleneck. Agents don't solve that part for you.

@llm_redteam @hasaamb This is the part everyone skips.

@AutoDS started as software I built to run my own 100 eBay stores.

I wasn't guessing who needed it. I was the customer. Build for a pain you actually have.

@AlexandraEnsgtr @arctic15 The one that stings is Gross Margin to Inference Cost.

You can't price your way out of a workflow that costs more to run than it earns.

We watch cost-per-action now like we watched CAC for years.

@johniosifov Per-seat was always a proxy.

Customers never wanted seats. They wanted the outcome the seat produced.

We learned this charging real money before building anything. People pay for the result, not the login.

@caffeineai The win here isn't the integration.

It's killing one context switch.

We rebuilt @AutoDS onboarding on the same idea: every extra step is a place users quit. Fewer steps beats more features.

@jackfriks I walked from a signed PE deal the day before closing.

The multiple was fine. The buyer wanted to gut the team.

Who owns it after matters more than the multiple. We picked @fiverr for alignment, not the top bid.

@rileybrown Agents can rebuild your features in 5 minutes.

They can't rebuild 9 years of trust, support, and knowing exactly why people churn.

I read every cancellation reason myself. That's the moat, not the code.

@romanbuildsaas Built @AutoDS from Beer Sheva, Israel.

No SF, no accent, no coffee-shop intros.

First 2-3 years, we had no website. 1M  customers later. Location helps. It's not the moat.

@eoghan @salesforce @fin_ai The line that matters here isn't the $3.6B.

It's "I'll still be CEO."

When we sold @AutoDS to @fiverr, that was the only term I actually cared about. Vision alignment beats the headline number.

@jasonlk Agreed.

It's not B2B dying, it's lazy software dying.

We survived because we read every churn response and solved the real pain, not the loudest feature request. AI raises the floor. It doesn't build trust.

@noelcetaSEO Free trials dropping makes sense.

Free users tell you they love it. Only paying users prove it.

When we test new products at @AutoDS, we charge real money first, then refund. The "will you pay" signal is the only one that counts.

@maxkolysh We hit $30K MRR in 6 months too.

Not from ads. From sitting in our competitor's groups every day, reading every complaint, and building the fix.

Founder-led distribution beats clever funnels early.

@Dhruvam987 The suspension at the end is the only realistic beat.

AI made building the easy part.

Not getting ignored, not getting shut down, and actually keeping users is still the whole game.

@StartupArchive_ This is why I walked away from a signed PE deal one day before closing.

The money was there. The buyer wanted to gut the team and run it as a cash cow.

When it stops being fun, the number stops mattering.

@Mr_Derivatives The market isn't punishing SaaS.

It's punishing software that any AI can rebuild in a weekend.

We went deep on one niche at @AutoDS for 9 years. That moat doesn't show up in a vibe-coded clone.

@gabriel1 A boring, slightly successful SaaS is worth more than it looks from the inside.

We had no website for the first 2-3 years. Just a login screen.

Sci-fi comes and goes. Boring recurring revenue compounds.

@StaniKulechov Building it once is easy now.

Maintaining it, supporting it, and improving it for years is the hard part.

Over 1M people paid @AutoDS instead of building their own. Not because they couldn't. Because they didn't want to own it.

@Finance_Weights Writing the code was never the bottleneck for us at @AutoDS.

We outshipped a funded competitor for years.

What slows everyone down is the stuff around the code. A bank has 10x more of that.

@ChaunceyGardner I agree on one thing 
What makes money 
Makes money 😉

@Seanfrank The product that needs three trends to sound interesting usually solves zero real problems.

Pick one painful thing. Go deep.

Trend-stacking is just the fear of commitment with a pitch deck.

@kseniam0s @perplexity_ai @GoogleDeepMind @claudeai @supabase @stripe @resend Solid stack.

The one thing I'd add isn't a tool: a way to talk to 10 real users before you write a line of code.

The cheapest item in any stack is the demand check everyone skips.

@AIFrontliner The shift from asking to delegating is the real unlock.

It's the same as managing people: if you check every output, you haven't actually delegated.

The goal is trusting the system enough to stop watching it.

@a16z The 1997 parallel holds: infrastructure cashed in last, vertical products cashed in first.

The winners won't own the biggest model.

They'll solve one painful problem for one specific buyer.

@DrSerunjogiEmma SaaS isn't dead. Generic SaaS is.

We ran @AutoDsTools for years with no website. Just a login screen and word of mouth.

Constraints don't slow you down. They force you to find what actually works.

@marclou 512 days to $20K MRR is the part nobody screenshots.

My first years were a 3 AM alarm to restart servers while everyone slept.

The grind is the story. The highlight reel is just editing.

@merishabh_singh I've hired over 300 people.

Almost none of my best ones came from a name-brand school.

The ones who message you before they're "qualified" are usually the ones who outwork everyone.

@ericosiu The agencies that win won't sell hours or AI.

They'll sell the outcome and let AI carry the cost.

Cutting headcount to protect margin is what you do when you're defending, not building.

@yunta_tsai The second type builds companies that last.

I still read every cancellation reason our users write.

You can delegate a lot. Understanding your own problem isn't one of them.

@gippp69 This is the real story in AI right now.

Not the smartest model. The cheapest one that finishes a whole job.

Margins like that turn a boring service into a business. That's where the money's hiding.

@I_vishnu_nair @rajeevrajr Headcount was never the goal. Output was.

We just used to confuse the two.

The best teams I've seen aren't the biggest. They're the ones with the least coordination overhead.

@repsilvio The shift from executor to manager is the whole thing.

I've run my company on one rule: if someone can do it 70% as well as I, they do it.

Turns out that rule works on agents, too.

@WhiteCollarExit This is the part people miss.

Agents don't rebuild your stack. They operate it.

The SaaS that wins makes itself the easiest thing for an agent to use. Same game as making it easy for a human.

@PodcastAlphaX @levie Seat pricing was always a proxy for value, not value itself.

Agents just exposed the gap.

Price the outcome, not the headcount. That shift was coming with or without agents.

@garrytan Commodification is good news for builders.

When the models flatten, the moat moves back to whoever solves one painful problem best.

That's a game founders can actually win.

@rakib_md007 The hard part isn't the tool. It's knowing exactly what you want.

Vague instructions in, vague output out.

It's why most delegations fail, too. The agent just exposes it faster.

@Tech_Rose1 Memory and evaluation are where most agents quietly die.

The model is rarely the real problem.

Same lesson we learned with people: the system around the talent beats the talent.

@starter_story Tiny tools win because they own one painful problem.

Every focused project I ran worked. Every "do everything" one failed.

Narrow isn't a limitation. It's the whole strategy.

@JamesJames518 This is exactly how I beat my biggest competitor.

I sat in their Facebook groups every day, reading what users hated.

Built every feature they begged for. The complaints were the roadmap.

@azamatbuilds Distribution is the moat. Always was.

@AutoDsTools had no website for the first 2 years. Just a login screen.

It won because I knew the niche better than anyone. Not because the tech was rare.

@cyrilXBT I've hired over 300 people.
I've never once hired off a certificate.

I hire off what someone shipped. A cert just prices a skill the market has already moved past.

@imjaredz The scrappy years are the asset, not the thing you graduate from.

In my first 2 years, I set a 3 AM alarm to restart servers myself.

Budget makes you comfortable. Constraints made me dangerous.

@brandonjcarl The model is the easy 10%.

The other 90% is data, security, and getting humans to change how they work.

The tech was never the bottleneck.

@RoundtableSpace This is how the best products start.

I built @AutoDsTools for my own 100 eBay stores first.

Other sellers saw it and wanted in. Solving your own pain is the only validation that matters.

@Suryanshti777 @gigacreate Right direction, but careful.

When we let users shape @AutoDsTools, the best signal came from what they hated, not what they requested.

Customers ask for features. They rarely name the real problem.

@petergyang The narrow ones solving one painful problem are the safest, not the weakest.

Nobody opens Claude to handle the 50 edge cases a niche workflow hides.

Generic SaaS competes with a prompt. Deep SaaS competes with nothing.

@pierreeliottlal Point 1 is the whole game.

We test new ideas by charging real money first, then refunding and adding people to a waitlist.

If they won't pay before it exists, the feature doesn't get built.

@samdblond Founder-led sales isn't a phase you graduate from.

I still read every churn survey at @AutoDsTools. Closing the first customers taught me what no deck ever could.

You can't delegate that early.

@OrevaZSN I've hired over 300 people.

We didn't stop hiring juniors when AI arrived; we changed what they did. 

The pipeline only breaks when you treat juniors as cheap output rather than future seniors.

@amitisinvesting Everyone called no-code the end of software, too.

We spent 9 years building @AutoDsTools. The moat was never the code; it was knowing every edge case a million users hit.

Models don't learn that for free.

The $10k month feels impossible right up until it's suddenly behind you.

@AutoDsTools did around $30k MRR in the first 6 months with no marketing site, just a login and a signup form.

The whole job back then was two questions: how do I get one more customer, and how do I make the product a little better. Everything else was noise.

@lagerskoy The build being cheap is exactly why it stopped being the moat.

Anyone can ship the canvas in 3 hours now.

We grew @AutoDsTools to 250 people by obsessing over why customers leave, not how fast we could deliver. The margin lives in retention, not the setup fee.

@itsmichaelluu 97% retention is the only number that matters.

9 years in, I still read every cancellation survey @AutoDsTools gets.

The multiple follows the product, never the other way around. When customers stop leaving, the re-rating takes care of itself.

@Aptos @_inceptioncap @davidgan1818 @aptAlix Matches our path exactly.

@AutoDsTools had no marketing site for the first 2-3 years. Just a login screen.

I focused on one thing: getting customers, building the best product. The demand carried the rest.

@lmrankhan The scaling trap is real.

After we hit PMF, the danger wasn't the market. It was me overbuilding and hiring ahead of revenue.

The skill that got you to PMF actively works against you at scale.

@hkarthik In the first 2 years of @AutoDS, I set a 3 AM alarm to restart servers.

The grind was real, but it wasn't the win. It was survival until we found what worked.

Grind without product-market fit is just an expensive motion.

@ShinkaIoT Outcome pricing is coming. But "the seat is dead" is too clean.

The winners will charge for outcomes and keep a floor, so revenue doesn't swing with a customer's agent count.

Pure usage pricing punishes you when your best customers become efficient.

@0xKiyoro The savings aren't the story. The pricing is.

Clients didn't pay 2.7x for cheaper infra. They paid because "your data never leaves" killed a fear.

You don't price features. Your price removed fear.

@gregisenberg The unlock isn't voice quality.

It's that the agent can finally handle the messy edge cases that a human used to catch.

For years, I read every cancellation reply at @AutoDS by hand. That's the work this can finally touch.

@NoLimitGains The companies that own the data don't get disrupted by agents.

They become the place where the agents go to work.

Every automation wave we rode at AutoDS made the core data more valuable, not less.

@edzitron We measure AI ROI per resolved support ticket and per minute saved on QA.

If your CFO can't draw that line, the AI bill is a vibe.

Most companies are still in the vibe phase.

@dee_bosa Already swapped one production workflow from a premium API to a cheaper open model.

Same quality. 80% cost cut.

Pricing power held up through the experiment phase. It's about to crack.

@gregisenberg "Agent debt" is the one I keep thinking about.

Already accumulating it: workflows wired to specific model quirks, prompts that only work on one vendor.

Nobody's pricing this in.

@BullTheoryio Yes. And the hidden number is the switching cost.

We tested 3 vendors this quarter. The switch took 2 weeks of engineering each time.

50x cheaper is real. So is the 2-week tax.

@bridgemindai This is the line in the sand.

The day a $5K laptop runs production-grade inference, API-only AI pricing breaks.

Already happening on the smaller use cases.

@anmol_biz The rule I broke for 3 years at AutoDS.

Priced for cost. Left $10M on the table before fixing it.

Customers told us we were a steal. That's not a compliment.

@lamacodes Both happen.

Shipped real product from vibe-coded prototypes, still running 6 months later.

The tool doesn't decide the outcome. Distribution does.

@moneyfet1sh Services pay the bills right now, no argument.

But an agency sells the same hour once.

We served over a million customers with software that kept earning while we slept. That's the part that compounds.

@DavidSacks Every tool that made our team faster created more to build, not less.

9 years in, cheaper code never once meant fewer engineers.

It meant more surface that someone actually has to understand.

How do you turn the worst mistake of your career into a personal brand win?

@lifeof_jer, CEO of @pocketosai , just gave a masterclass in building in public when it breaks.

Here's the story:

@zuess05 Hot take, but wrong direction.

We scaled to 250 people. The bottleneck was never too many PMs.

It was PMs who couldn't say no to features.

@ThePrimeagen "Codify every rule" is the underrated part.

We spent two weeks writing our internal standards before letting AI touch our codebase.

The teams that skipped that step are still cleaning up.

@arvidkahl The documents folder approach is exactly right.

We learned this the hard way. AI without context builds fast and breaks everything.

Context-first, code-second. Same rule we use for onboarding new engineers.

@_Investinq I've hired 300  people over 9 years.

The best engineers weren't the best coders. They were the ones who could explain what they built.

Communication was always the moat. AI just made it obvious.

@willahmed We went from 50 to 250 people while automating half our workflows.

AI didn't replace headcount. It changed what we hire for.

Less manual ops. More product thinkers.

@wallstengine Ran this evaluation ourselves. Enterprise buyers don't want LLM lock-in.

Whoever owns the process layer wins. 
Not the model layer.

@om_patel5 We coordinate AI across multiple products with 250 people.

49 agents sounds great until agent #37 contradicts agent #12 at 3 AM.

The coordination layer is where everything breaks.

@ujjwalscript Hired over 300 people in 9 years. The ones who stood out never listed skills.

They showed me a problem they solved and what it saved.

That was the whole interview.

@farzyness Same. 250 people, busier than ever.

AI didn't cut the work. 
It raised the bar for what counts as work.

@dee_bosa After the Fiverr acquisition, I watched public e-commerce companies scramble to show growth numbers.

Most of them bought time, not growth.

The ones still standing built products people actually need. Everything else is financial theater.

@rohanpaul_ai I started at 14, buying phone cases for $10 and selling them for $50 on eBay.

No AI. No tools. Just arbitrage and Wi-Fi.

Now imagine a 14-year-old with these tools. The gap between idea and revenue just went from years to hours.

@steipete $852B valuation. Zero profit.

Meanwhile, there are bootstrapped SaaS companies achieving $10M in ARR, 90% margins, and 3 employees.

The most valuable AI companies in 10 years might be the ones nobody is funding today.

@ThePrimeagen Our best engineers don't write more code with AI.

They ship more products.

The metric changed from lines of code to features per week.

@heyibinance We hired an AI consultant at AutoDS last year.

Not to replace people. To make 250 employees do what used to take 400.

The shift isn't "AI does my job." It's "AI makes my job worth 2x more."

@ArthurMacwaters Built AutoDS for 8 years with zero funding.

No pitch deck. No investor meetings. Just customers paying every month.

The "right way" is whichever way keeps you alive long enough to win.

@MatthewBerman We have 100 engineers at AutoDS.

All of them use Claude Code.

The "secret" isn't in the source files. It's in how you structure the prompts that your team feeds it.

@Polymarket $1M/day is wild. 

Building a product people love to try is easy. 
Building one they pay for monthly is a completely different game.

@Pirat_Nation Downloads without retention. The most expensive vanity metric in tech.

We track one number at AutoDS: Do sellers come back tomorrow?

If the answer is no, nothing else matters. Sora had a million users. It never had a million use cases.

@Akintola_steve We had a version of this at AutoDS.

The feature went viral on a forum. Traffic 10x'd overnight. The system couldn't handle it.

That week we stopped celebrating growth and started stress-testing for it. Painful lesson, but it only needs to happen once.

@hthieblot Was exactly this founder at 21.

eBay banned my account. Zero revenue overnight. Took a loan to cover a tax bill I didn't know existed.

Built a mentorship program the same month to survive. That desperation became the muscle that built everything after.

@t_blom Already happening at companies I know.

One founder told me his Claude API bill passed his first engineer's salary 4 months ago.

The question isn't whether it happens. It's whether the ROI justifies it. For most use cases we've tested, it does. By a lot.

@thedealdirector 9 years selling SaaS in e-commerce.

The pitch hasn't changed. "Here's your problem. Here's how we fix it. Here's proof."

What changed: buyers now test 3 AI alternatives before your demo call even starts. If your product doesn't show value in 10 minutes, you lose.

@trikcode Saw this happen with our 100 engineers.

AI made the fast people faster. It made the slow people feel overwhelmed.

The gap didn't shrink. It tripled.

@theo Started AutoDS at 21.

9 years later, 250 employees, exit to a public company.

Being a founder isn't easier. It's just the only option when you're too impatient to wait for permission.

AI compute demand got so intense, we're moving servers to space.

@nvidia-backed Starcloud raised $170M to build an 88,000-satellite constellation for AI data centers.

When the power grid can't keep up, you leave the planet.

@RoundtableSpace The same thing happened in e-commerce 3 years ago.

AI started generating product listings and images at scale.

Sellers who moved first 10x'd their catalog overnight.

@atulkumarzz Tested 6 AI agents for customer support last quarter.

5 needed more babysitting than the humans they replaced.

The one that worked did one thing. But did it perfectly.

@coreyganim The math works.

But we validated every product by selling it first. Then building.

Find 10 people who'll pay before you write a line of code.

@RoundtableSpace 250 people across multiple products.

The bottleneck was never the tools.

It was the context stuck in people's heads that never made it into any system.

@SuryaUthkarsha @ycombinator @realthomasgu @richardzphotoz @jonathanzliu Started my first business at 14. Flipped phones from China on eBay.

21 years later, same energy.

Keep going.

@unusual_whales We track AI prompt usage per team, per week.

Not to monitor. To see who's adapting.

Nicotine isn't a productivity system. Curiosity is.

@swyx Shipped 47 features last year.

Users remember three of them.

The ones that worked on day one.

@tankots We serve sellers in 50  countries.

The #1 request is never "make it smarter."

Always "make it simpler."

@bcherny 100 engineers on my team.

The ones shipping fastest aren't the best coders.

They're the ones who learned to prompt.

@DataChaz The feature most teams sleep on: persistent memory.

We set it up so Claude remembers our pricing logic, competitor data, and product roadmap across sessions.

New hires are onboarded through Claude before talking to a human. Saved us 2 weeks per hire

@ivanburazin This is exactly why we build AI that does the work FOR the user, not AI that gives them more work to do.

Our sellers don't want to create content. They want sales.

The products that win give results, not tools.

@HedgieMarkets Klarna bragged about replacing 700 people with a chatbot.

Then customer satisfaction tanked, and they started quietly rehiring.

We went the other direction. Kept all 250 people, added AI to their workflows. Revenue per employee up 40%.

In February, 3 companies absorbed 83% of all global VC.

@OpenAI raised $110B. @Anthropic closed a $30B round.

If you're building outside that orbit, you're playing a different game.

@OpenAI just backed a $650M startup whose only job is to coordinate AI agents.

Not build models. Not train data. Just coordinate agents.

The bottleneck shifted.

@gregisenberg We run both in production.

Opus for architecture decisions. Codex for rapid iteration.

Choosing one over the other is like choosing between a strategist and a sprinter. You need both on the team.

@gregisenberg We killed our own pricing model twice in 4 years.

Hurt like hell both times. Revenue dipped for 2 months.

Then it grew 3x because we stopped protecting what was comfortable and started pricing for what customers actually wanted.

@gregisenberg 3 years ago, we hired 12 full-time content people.

Today we have 4 people and 6 agents doing 3x the output.

The skill that matters now isn't managing people. It's designing the system where humans and agents actually work together.

@gregisenberg When we scaled from 50 to 250 people, the hardest lesson was that more people don't mean more output.

We restructured 3 teams last year around AI workflows.

Same output. 40% fewer meetings. Zero layoffs. That's the path companies skip.

@startupandrew We tested this with internal tools at AutoDS.

Replaced 3 dashboards with one agent that pulls exactly what each team needs.

Nobody missed the UI. They missed the answers. Now they get those faster.

@jasonlk Saw this firsthand at AutoDS.

We built our moat on data and workflow, not on the model.

The moment your product is just a wrapper around an API, you're one prompt away from being replaced.

@gregisenberg We went from 30 engineers on a feature to 5 engineers plus agents shipping the same thing in half the time.

The bottleneck moved from code to context.

The founders who actually talk to customers every day are winning now.

@rauchg The same thing happened to us.

We replaced 3 internal SaaS tools with custom-built alternatives in just a few weeks. 
Better fit, fraction of the cost.

@JLarky We've had @GitHub go down during critical deploys.

Not once did anyone suggest switching.

When your moat is the entire dev ecosystem, availability becomes a feature request, not a dealbreaker.

@SemiAnalysis_ Seeing this in our own numbers.

We cut AI cost per action by 60%. Usage went up 5x.

Jevons Paradox is the best thing that happened to SaaS margins in a decade.

@garrytan We've been shipping AI-generated features to production for months.

Not patches. Full modules.

The gap between "this is overhyped" and "we're already doing it" is about 6 months.

@Yuchenj_UW We've seen this firsthand, managing 100 engineers.

The best ones use AI to ship 3x faster. The average ones use it to look busy.

AI is a multiplier. It multiplies whatever you already are.

@AlexFinn For SaaS founders this is the real headline: AI cost per user just dropped again.

We've watched our compute costs fall 4x in 12 months while output quality went up.

The margin story for AI-native SaaS keeps getting better.

@ostrisai @Lightricks @ltx_model Fine-tuning used to take a team and a month.

Now it takes one developer and an afternoon.

The barrier to custom AI just disappeared.

@business 10,000 AI chips for one startup.

The AI race isn't about models anymore.

It's about who controls the compute.

@Pirat_Nation Capcom just turned "no AI" into a brand differentiator.

In 2026, "100% human-made" is a premium label.

Smart positioning.

@heyrimsha Most people blame the model when prompts fail.

Karpathy just proved the model wasn't the problem.

The instructions were.

@charliejhills This changes everything about AI coding.

The problem was never intelligence. It was amnesia.

Now it learns how YOU work. That's the real unlock.

@deedydas Anonymity online was always an illusion.

AI just made it obvious.

Your writing style is a fingerprint. Always was.
